#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:1
msgid "Pipeline Comparison++ (Quantitative)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:2
msgid "TL;DR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:3
msgid ""
"Across 1000 blinded animator comparisons, RP++, Rich++, and Robust++ show"
" consistent preference gains over legacy pipelines, particularly when ≥20"
" calibration profiles are available. Improvements scale with additional "
"actor-specific data, indicating better identity adaptation rather than "
"baseline tracking changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:5
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:7
msgid ""
"In 25.10 we released a couple new pipelines (the ones with `++' suffix) ."
" For those who are interested, we switched to a new facial tracking "
"backend, which provides much stabler and accurate results compared to our"
" previous backend, especially on close-up images (e.g. from a head-"
"mounted camera). We ironed out some bugs in the pipeline in 26.04, and "
"properly tested whether and how the new tracking pipeline outperform the "
"old ones, and we would love to share it with you if you were interested."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:9
msgid ""
"In this page we refer to retarget profiles as individual image of the "
"actor and the corresponding character, rigged to express the same facial "
"expression as the actor in the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:11
msgid ""
"The quality of animation is a complex concept, first and foremost, it is "
"highly subjective; but generally speaking, a few main factors impact the "
"quality of final animation, including:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:12
msgid ""
"the quality of the facial tracking (how accurately the facial tracking "
"captures the actor's facial expression),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:13
msgid ""
"the quality of the retarget profiles (how well your profile actually "
"matches the actor),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:14
msgid ""
"the quantity of facial retarget profiles (how many profiles are used to "
"retarget the animation),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:15
msgid ""
"the quality of the extrapolation method (how well can the algorithm uses "
"your retarget profiles to explain the facial movement of the actor)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:17
msgid ""
"This update focuses on the quality of the facial tracking. While there "
"are well-established, deterministic metrics that we used internally to "
"evaluate the quality of the facial tracking, we found that they do not "
"necessarily provide a good and reliable indication of the quality of the "
"final animation oftentimes. As such, we often resort to subjective "
"evaluation by professional animators, and today we would like to share "
"the results of our evaluation with you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:21
msgid "Methodology"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:22
msgid ""
"The subjective evaluation is done by a set of in-house professional "
"animators (Raters)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:24
msgid ""
"Raters are shown a pair of animations, generated by different pipelines, "
"together with the original performance video. Raters are asked to pick "
"the animation that more faithfully recreates the actor's facial "
"expression in a short clip. Raters were not told which pipeline generated"
" each animation. Presentation order (left/right) was randomized. The "
"total data size is 1000 comparisons, and we have 5 professional raters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:26
msgid ""
"**Clip selection.** 5-second clips were chosen randomly from an internal "
"corpus representative of production data, captured using head-mounted "
"cameras."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:28
msgid ""
"**Rater instructions.** Pick the animation that more faithfully recreates"
" the actor's facial expression; the original performance video is shown "
"as reference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:30
msgid "**Replay.** Raters may replay clips as many times as they wish."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:32
msgid ""
"**Presentation.** Clips are shown side-by-side, with the reference "
"performance video displayed in the center or flanking both sides (rater's"
" choice)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:34
msgid ""
"**Pipeline selection.** Pipeline are randomly selected from the following"
" list: RP, Rich, Robust, RP++, Rich++, Robust++, however, Robust is not "
"widely used in production, so it receives fewer comparisons."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:36
msgid ""
"**Profile selection.**  We assume >20 profiles from a Range of Motion "
"(ROM) set, between 0 to 100 profiles from other videos (accumulated from "
"earlier stage of production), and between 0 to 5 profiles from the video "
"that is being processed. This closely mimics different production stages"
"  where earlier only a small number of profiles are available, and later "
"more profiles are available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:38
msgid ""
"Each comparison is evaluated by a single rater. In a smaller-scale pilot "
"study with overlapping assignments, inter-rater agreement was high."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:40
msgid ""
"This study focuses primarily on legacy pipelines and their new++ "
"counterparts.  In addition, we also look into which pipeline performs "
"best when more profiles are available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:43
msgid ""
"Legacy Robust receives fewer comparisons as it is not widely used in "
"production."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:45
msgid "Results"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:47
#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:49
msgid "Win Matrix"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:49
msgid "![Win Matrix](../assets/win_matrix.jpeg)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:51
msgid ""
"We show the raw results of comparison between each of the pipelines. "
"Overall, it is easy to see that our new pipelines, RP++, Rich++ and "
"Robust++ tends to outperform their legacy counterpart."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:53
msgid ""
"Note that this matrix aggregates results across all profile counts. The "
"Bradley-Terry model below disentangles the effect of profile count on "
"pipeline performance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:55
msgid "Bradley-Terry Model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:57
#, python-format
msgid ""
"Additionally, we model the performance of the pipelines against each "
"other via the [Bradley–Terry "
"model](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley%E2%80%93Terry_model)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:59
msgid "The performance of the pipeline is modeled as follow:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:61
msgid ""
"\n"
"logit(P(pipe_a > pipe_b | s)) = (\\beta_a - \\beta_b) + (\\phi+ "
"(\\delta_a - \\delta_b)) log(1+s)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:65
msgid ""
"where $P(pipe_a > pipe_b)$ represents the probability of pipeline a being"
" selected when being presented with pipeline b, $\\beta$ is the base "
"performance of the pipeline, $s$ is the number of profiles, $\\phi$ is "
"the shared, global dependence on $s$, and $\\delta$ is the marginal "
"effect of additional data to the performance of the pipeline."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md
msgid "Term"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md
msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md
msgid "β"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md
msgid "intrinsic solver quality with minimal data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md
msgid "δ"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md
msgid "how efficiently solver converts additional data into quality"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md
msgid "φ"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md
msgid "global diminishing-returns effect"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:73
msgid "Performance vs. Profile Count"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:75
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"After solving for $\\beta, \\delta, \\text{and } \\phi$, we can visualize"
" the performance of each of the algorithm at different profile count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:77
msgid "![Performance](../assets/performance_over_profiles.jpeg)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:77
msgid "Performance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:79
msgid ""
"The y-axis represents estimated Bradley-Terry strength, where legacy "
"Robust is anchored at 0 as the baseline. Higher values indicate stronger "
"rater preference. The value corresponds to $\\beta + \\delta \\cdot "
"\\log(1+s)$ from the model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:81
msgid ""
"From the graph, the performance of all the new pipelines are on par with "
"our previous reference pipeline RP. The performance gap enlarges between "
"the legacy pipeline and the new pipeline when more profiles are added to "
"the session."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:83
msgid ""
"Legacy Robust serves as the reference baseline in the Bradley-Terry model"
" (its strength is fixed at 0). It was designed for multi-actor sessions —"
" a use case that violates the single-actor assumption shared by the other"
" pipelines — which explains its low relative performance in this single-"
"actor evaluation. See [Pipeline Comparison](002_pipeline_comparison.md) "
"for details on when Robust is appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:85
msgid "Conclusion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../experiments/003_pipeline_comparison_quantitative.md:87
msgid ""
"Across 1000 blinded animator comparisons, the new pipeline generation "
"(RP++, Rich++, Robust++) shows consistent preference gains over legacy "
"pipelines, with improvements scaling as more calibration profiles are "
"available."
msgstr ""

